content.text:
Now check base.css for body/background transition rules and index.html inline script (theme applied before paint). Also check if there's any existing transition for theme.

Plan: add a smooth color transition when theme changes. Common approach: add a `.theme-transition` class to html, with global transition on color/background-color/border-color/fill/stroke/box-shadow for ~400ms, then remove the class after the transition. Applying globally permanently can cause performance issues and weird transitions on hover; and can break animations (e.g., transitions on transform). Better approach: a "theme switching" class added on toggle, with CSS like:

```css
html.theme-switching *,
html.theme-switching *::before,
html.theme-switching *::after {
  transition: background-color .45s var(--ease-out), border-color .45s ..., color .35s ..., fill .35s, stroke .35s, box-shadow .45s !important;
  transition-delay: 0s !important;
}
```

But `!important` overrides component transitions (e.g., hover backgrounds) — only during the 450ms switch, acceptable.

Then remove the class after transition. But if a component has its own transition property, our rule with !important replaces it. On removal, transitions revert.

Alternative modern approach: View Transitions API (`document.startViewTransition`) — smooth cross-fade between theme snapshots. Chrome/Edge support; Safari 18 has it; Firefox recent 144? As of 2026, Firefox supports View Transitions? Firefox 144 maybe. Could use progressive enhancement with fallback to the CSS class transition. That gives a beautiful cross-fade of the whole page, including gradients, images? Note view transitions freeze rendering and cross-fade.

But crossfade of whole page can look like a flash and also crossfades text (a bit blurry). Many sites use a radial "theme wipe" with View Transitions (like a circle expanding from the toggle button). That could be nice with this expressive design: circular clip-path reveal from the theme button. Implement `startViewTransition` with `::view-transition-new(root)` clip-path animation. Fallback: CSS color transition.

However, theme persistence useEffect sets dataset.theme asynchronously after render. To use View Transitions, we need to wrap the state update in startViewTransition so DOM change happens inside callback:

```tsx
const applyTheme = (next) => {
  if (!document.startViewTransition) { setThemeState(next); return; }
  const transition = document.startViewTransition(() => {
    flushSync(() => setThemeState(next));  // or just set state
  });
}
```

But the DOM attribute is set in useEffect, after the React commit — startViewTransition's callback resolves after update callback returns, and React's setState is async (batched). We'd need `flushSync` from react-dom plus set the dataset synchronously inside callback. Actually simpler: set `document.documentElement.dataset.theme` directly in the toggle inside the startViewTransition callback, and let the effect also set it. But then React's effect runs later—same value, no-op.

Alternatively, set the dataset synchronously in `setThemeState` wrapper before startViewTransition's callback. Hmm.

Simplest robust implementation: do the DOM attribute + meta + localStorage in the setter synchronously (inside the view transition callback), and keep React state for UI. But provider currently applies in useEffect — that's fine generally.

Given this is a design-forward site, a circular reveal from the toggle button is attractive. But user asked "сделай плавный переход" — smooth transition. A cross-fade via View Transitions is the cleanest.

Design considerations:
- The site uses warm material design. A radial wipe from the theme button fits "Material 3 Expressive".
- Need to respect prefers-reduced-motion.
- Fallback for browsers without View Transitions: global color transition class technique.

Implementation plan:

1. In `ThemeProvider.tsx`:
   - Create `applyTheme(next: Theme)` function used by toggle/setTheme:
     ```tsx
     const commitTheme = useCallback((next: Theme) => {
       const root = document.documentElement;
       const reduce = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
       if (reduce || typeof document.startViewTransition !== 'function') {
         setThemeState(next);
         return;
       }
       const transition = document.startViewTransition(() => {
         flushSync(() => setThemeState(next));
       });
       ...
     }, []);
     ```
     But flushSync and effect ordering: `useEffect` that writes dataset runs after paint? React's useEffect runs after commit, synchronously after paint flush in flushSync? flushSync flushes effects synchronously I believe (useEffect inside flushSync is flushed before returning? Actually flushSync flushes passive effects before returning? React docs: flushSync may force pending effects to run). To be safe, set the dataset directly in the callback:
     ```tsx
     const apply = (next: Theme) => {
       root.dataset.theme = next;
       syncMeta(next);
       persist(next);
     };
     ```
     Then `setThemeState(next)` — the effect will re-apply idempotently.

     But the transition snapshot: startViewTransition captures the old state when called; the callback mutates DOM; the new snapshot is taken after callback's promise resolves. If we mutate the attribute synchronously inside the callback, the new snapshot captures it. 

   - Use `view-transition-name: none` for most, and style in CSS:
     ```css
     ::view-transition-old(root),
     ::view-transition-new(root) { animation: none; mix-blend-mode: normal; }
     ::view-transition-old(root) { z-index: 1; }
     ::view-transition-new(root) { z-index: 2; animation: theme-wipe .55s var(--ease-out); }
     ```
     Circular reveal from the toggle button:
     ```css
     @keyframes theme-wipe { from { clip-path: circle(0 at var(--theme-x) var(--theme-y)); } to { clip-path: circle(var(--theme-r) at ...); } }
     ```
     Compute x,y,r at click time and set CSS vars on root. But `toggle` may be called elsewhere (not just button) — there's only ThemeButton in Header + mobile menu. Both call toggle. To get coords, we can listen for toggle with an optional event? `toggle` has no args. Could compute from the active element (`document.activeElement` — the button was just clicked, so it's the clicked button) via getBoundingClientRect. Nice trick: use `(document.activeElement as HTMLElement)?.getBoundingClientRect()`. But buttons get focus on click in Chrome; in Safari clicking a button doesn't focus it. The theme button is a button; could pass coordinates explicitly.

     Simpler and robust: expanding circle from the top-right corner? Or just crossfade. The user asked "плавный переход" — crossfade is smooth and safe. But a wipe is fancier. Risk of complexity/bugs.

