Insert theme transition block before the reduced-motion media, and add VT disable within reduced motion? Since JS skips VT under reduced motion, not needed, but system setting can change? It's checked at click time, so fine. Still add a safety CSS rule under reduced motion for view transitions.

CSS:

```css
/* ---------- theme switch ---------- */

/* View Transitions: the new palette spreads from the toggle button */
::view-transition-old(root),
::view-transition-new(root) {
  animation: none;
  mix-blend-mode: normal;
}

::view-transition-old(root) {
  z-index: 1;
}

::view-transition-new(root) {
  z-index: 2;
  clip-path: circle(0% at var(--theme-x, 100%) var(--theme-y, 0%));
  animation: theme-reveal 560ms var(--ease-out) both;
}

@keyframes theme-reveal {
  to {
    clip-path: circle(var(--theme-r, 150vmax) at var(--theme-x, 100%) var(--theme-y, 0%));
  }
}

/* fallback for browsers without View Transitions: ease the palette */
html.theme-switching *,
html.theme-switching *::before,
html.theme-switching *::after {
  transition-property: background-color, border-color, color, fill, stroke, box-shadow, outline-color, text-decoration-color !important;
  transition-duration: 400ms !important;
  transition-timing-function: var(--ease-out) !important;
  transition-delay: 0s !important;
}
```

Hmm, `html.theme-switching` itself also needs transition for body? body is matched by `*`. Root background is on body, so covered.

Wait — the fallback class rule applies only when class on html; it selects descendants. Good. Add also `html.theme-switching` itself? html has no bg. Skip.

Potential: `transition-property` with `!important` overrides `.reveal` transitions during switch causing reveal elements to lose transform transition for 400ms — but reveal delay/duration reset. If a reveal occurs exactly then, it'll jump. Rare.

Also note `.cursor-dot` has `transition` with width/height etc.; during 400ms fallback it would only transition the listed properties, losing width/height transition. Rare and brief. Fine.

For view transitions, old/new root pseudo: with `animation: none` on old, new has the reveal. The default UA has `::view-transition-new(root) { animation: ... }` — our `animation` shorthand overrides.

But the view-transition pseudo elements also have `::view-transition-image-pair(root)` etc. No changes needed.

One question: default root has `mix-blend-mode: plus-lighter`? Actually the UA sets `::view-transition-old(root), ::view-transition-new(root) { mix-blend-mode: plus-lighter; }`? I recall the default is `mix-blend-mode: normal` for old? Chrome's default CSS includes `mix-blend-mode: plus-lighter` for images to avoid flashes. Setting `mix-blend-mode: normal` on both is the standard snippet. But if new snapshot is clipped, blending doesn't matter. Good.

Icons/fonts may re-render slightly differently in snapshot; fine.

Add `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` inside the theme block? Global rule already sets animation-duration 0.01ms for pseudo-elements? It uses `*, *::before, *::after` — view-transition pseudo-elements are not matched by element selectors? They are pseudo-elements, likely `*::before` doesn't match `::view-transition-new`. Add explicit:

```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  ::view-transition-group(*),
  ::view-transition-old(*),
  ::view-transition-new(*) {
    animation: none !important;
  }
}
```

Could put inside existing media block at the end. Note `::view-transition-group(*)` selector with universal inside is valid.

Add to existing reduced-motion media as well.

