content.text:
Now design the interaction. "сделай чтобы заголовок плавал и реагировал на мышь" — make the title float (idle animation) and react to the mouse (parallax/tilt based on cursor position). The hero title is two lines: "exDesktop" and "Telegram по-своему" (accent). The title spans have `hero-enter` class with entrance animation ending at `transform: none`; adding a float animation would conflict with the entrance animation (`animation` property). We need to compose.

Approach:
- Create a `HeroTitle` component (or add logic in Hero) that applies a mouse-following transform to the title lines. Use pointermove on the hero section or window, updating CSS vars `--tx/--ty` on the title container or per line, with per-line depth for a layered parallax feel. Add a constant idle float via CSS keyframes with different phases per line.
- Conflict: `hero-enter` animation on the spans. The `hero-enter` animation ends with `transform: none` and `animation-fill-mode: forwards`. If we add a second animation for floating on the same elements, the last animation wins for the transform property. Better: restructure — keep entrance animation on the wrapping `<h1>` or on inner elements, and apply float/mouse transform to a wrapper.

Structure idea:
```tsx
<h1 className="display hero-title">
  <span className="hero-title__line hero-title__line--a">
    <span className="hero-title__inner">{titleA}</span>
  </span>
  ...
</h1>
```
CSS: line wrapper gets perspective/translate from CSS vars; inner span animates float and entrance? Multiple transforms nested.

Cleaner: Use nested elements:
- `<h1 className="display hero-title" ref={titleRef}>` 
- each line: `<span className="hero-title__line"><span className="hero-title__word hero-enter">{...}</span></span>`

The entrance (`hero-enter`) applies opacity + translateY on the inner word. The float + mouse parallax applies translate3d/rotate on the line wrapper via CSS variables. Since transforms are on different elements, they compose visually.

But `hero-enter` animation ends with `transform: none`; the float animation would need to be on a different element than the entrance. Put the float on the line wrapper (parent) and entrance on the inner span? Then the line wrapper's transform (float+parallax) works independently, inner span animates entrance once.

Line wrapper needs `display: block` for both, with `will-change: transform`.

Mouse reaction: Which container listens? The whole hero section (or `.hero-copy`). A pointer listener on the hero section that computes normalized pointer position relative to the viewport/center and sets CSS vars on the title (e.g., `--mx: -0.5..0.5`). Then each line translates by different multipliers and rotates slightly.

Idle float: CSS keyframes `title-float` translateY(-4px) rotate? and slight skew? With `animation: title-float 7s ease-in-out infinite alternate` on the line wrapper. Then the mouse transform can't be on the same element (animation overrides transform). So three layers:
1. `.hero-title__line` — mouse parallax via `transform: translate3d(calc(var(--mx)*...), ...) rotate(...)`; CSS transition for smoothing.
2. `.hero-title__float` — idle keyframes translateY.
3. `.hero-title__word` (inner) — entrance hero-enter.

That's 3 spans per line. Alternatively, apply idle float via `translate` property (CSS `translate: 0 ...`) and mouse via `transform`, since `translate`/`rotate`/`scale` are independent properties from `transform`. Modern Chrome supports individual transform properties. We can animate `translate` with keyframes and set `transform` via inline style/CSS vars for mouse parallax. That reduces wrappers. But @keyframes animating `translate` while `transform` transitions works. Browser support: Chrome 104+, Safari 14.1+, Firefox 72+. Fine.

So:
```tsx
<h1 className="display hero-title" ref={titleRef}>
  <span className="hero-title__line" style={{ '--depth': 1 }}>
    <span className="hero-title__word hero-enter" ...>{titleA}</span>
  </span>
  ...
</h1>
```
Wait, if `.hero-title__line` uses `translate` animation for float and `transform` for parallax, and the inner word uses `hero-enter` transform. But then the mouse parallax and float are on the same element but different properties — works.

