All `.material` usages are inside Tilt. Great. So we can globally restyle `.material` to be opaque and remove the blur, without a `.tilt` scoping needed — since all uses are inside tilts. But `.material` might be used elsewhere in docs? grep shows only these. Good.

However, the design may rely on the glass look for these cards. Making them opaque (but still tonal) preserves the look mostly and fixes the artifacts. Do it:

In base.css `.material`:
```css
.material {
  background-color: var(--glass-fill);
  ...
  backdrop-filter: var(--glass-blur);
}
```
Override:
```css
/* tilted surfaces must be opaque: transparent/backdrop layers shimmer at the rounded edges while the card transforms */
.material {
  background-color: color-mix(in oklab, var(--surface-1) 62%, var(--bg));
  backdrop-filter: none;
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: none;
}
```
But `.material` is defined in base.css; changing it there affects all three. We could change the `.material` rule itself. Keep `.card` for the builder (already opaque via `.builder`). Other `.card`s (showcase? `.builder` only) fine.

